          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My dad is 80, confused since this morning and has not passed water since yesterday. How urgent is this, and what would be looked for?",
          "answer": "**Please get medical help now, today, not later.** A sudden change in thinking (confusion since this morning) combined with no urine for about a day in an 80-year-old needs same-day assessment. If he becomes very drowsy or hard to wake, has trouble breathing, chest pain, weakness on one side, or seems to be getting worse quickly, call emergency services (999 in the UK, 911 in the US) or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait for a routine appointment.\n\nThe triage check returned a \"routine\" result, but it said that no listed pattern matched and that this is not a clearance. I would not rely on it for this combination of symptoms.\n\nThe retrieved guidance does not describe what doctors look for in sudden confusion or in someone not passing urine. The only related passage is from the educational summary \"Urinary tract infection.\" It says that in a confused older person, a positive urine test is not on its own a diagnosis, and that the underlying cause needs to be found. It does not cover other causes, examinations, or tests, so I can't tell you more from these sources.\n\nWhen you speak to a clinician, it helps to mention when the confusion started, that he has not passed urine since yesterday, any fever, recent falls or injuries, what medicines he takes, and any known conditions such as kidney, prostate or heart problems.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
